<p>Thank you for the feedback, as well as the link! I was not familiar with that story, but I see what you mean; the test logs are very reminiscent!</p>
Regarding a rewrite with focus more on the note:
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I hope the following doesn't come off as defensive &#8212; as I accept that you might be right! &#8212; but I want to wait a little bit and see how the article fares before making any decisions about heavy rewrites.</p>
<p>I gave the note less focus because I felt this would maximize its impact; by the time you reach it, you've probably forgotten the circumstances under which SCP-3041 was discovered, and you've been reading a fairly clinical (albeit grotesque) account wherein SCP-3041's <em>actual</em> properties are revealed&#8230; the note is meant to sucker-punch you out of no where by taking that new knowledge and making it suddenly, deeply personal. In other words, I'm skeptical whether or not the note would have the same impact without the test logs and memo preceding it!</p>
<p>I also don't think I could expand this into a tale; I just don't feel like there's enough for me to tease out of it (though someone else more than welcome to try!). If it can't stand on its own as an SCP (and I'm willing to accept that it might not), I'll just dump it and offer the concept up to anyone else who wants to take a stab at it (no pun intended).</p>
</div>
